 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 5,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE FAIRY KNOWE. Locals say a ring of stones is a doorway if you stand in the middle at dusk and mind your manners. The sign is not official. The manners are."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "THE FAIRY POOLS (Glen Brittle). A stream tumbles down the glen in a staircase of clear blue-green pools. The water is about 8 degrees all year. Brave people swim. Sensible people take a photo and have a hot chocolate."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE SKYE BRIDGE. Opened in 1995, with tolls until 2004, when very cross campaigners got them scrapped. It hops over a little island, Eilean Bàn, which has a lighthouse and a rather smug otter."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "AN t-EILEAN SGITHEANACH · THE ISLE OF SKYE. Gaelic for 'the winged island', named for the shape of its peninsulas, or the Cuillin, or a Viking nickname. Everyone has a theory. Fàilte."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "THE OLD MAN OF STORR. A tall, thin pinnacle of rock about 50 metres high, left behind by an ancient landslip. It's still standing. So is the fog. Bring a coat, whatever the forecast says."
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "EILEAN DONAN. Just over the water from Skye, a castle on a tiny island where three sea lochs meet. Blown to bits by the Royal Navy in 1719 and rebuilt in the 1900s, it has starred in more films than most actors."
  }
 ],
